      "level": 1,
      "content": "Method Overriding"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "content": "1class Animal {\n2    void makeSound() {\n3        System.out.println(\"Animal Sound\");\n4    }\n5}\n6\n7class Dog extends Animal {\n8    void makeSound() {\n9        System.out.println(\"Woof Woof\");\n10    }\n11}\n12\n13class Main {\n14    public static void main(String[] args) {\n15\n16        Dog dog1 = new Dog();\n17        dog1.makeSound();\n18    }\n19}\n20\n21// Output: Woof Woof"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, the makeSound() method is present in both the Animal and the Dog classes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "However, when we call the method using the object of dog1, the method of the Dog class is executed."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This is because the method in the child class (Dog) overrides the same method in the parent class (Animal). And, this process is known as method overriding."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Dog class overrides the makeSound() method of superclass Animal",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
